      en: "The Tin Can Island Port in Lagos was officially commissioned in 1977 to alleviate the massive congestion at the neighboring Apapa Port during Nigeria's oil boom. The construction was completed in a record time of just 15 months, representing a significant engineering achievement at the time. The port specializes in container handling and the importation of vehicles through its modern Roll-on/Roll-off (RoRo) terminals. It encompasses several terminals managed by private operators, which has led to increased operational efficiency and improved service delivery. Geographically, the port is situated on an island separated from the main Lagos lagoon by waterways, featuring deep-water channels that accommodate large ocean-going vessels. Tin Can Island is a vital component of Nigeria's maritime infrastructure and contributes substantially to the country's customs revenue. Despite its modern facilities, the port faces ongoing logistical challenges due to road congestion on its access routes. It remains an indispensable hub for the import of consumer goods and industrial raw materials into the Nigerian market.",
